#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
verify.py — the registered numerical checks for the deterministic Adler
calibration and the analytic flat-spectrum control.

Run:

    python3 -m adler_born_two_channel.verify
    python3 -m adler_born_two_channel.verify --verbose
    python3 adler_born_two_channel/verify.py

Every check reports a measured numerical residual and the tolerance it was held
to, so a "pass" is auditable rather than a bare assertion.  The script exits 0
only if every check passes.

This file is the *only* place allowed to see both the dynamics and the analytic
prediction.  That is the point: comparing a measured relaxation rate against a
predicted one has to happen somewhere, and it happens here, outside the code
that generates trajectories.  Check 12 parses ``model.py`` and ``simulate.py``
to confirm they never reach across that line themselves.

What these checks establish
---------------------------
That the Adler fixed point, the near-lock relaxation rate, the phase-slip
frequency outside the tongue, the raised-cosine envelope, the tongue entry and
exit times, and the finite-time lag are all what the equations say they are; and
that the flat-spectrum rate sum converges to (pi/2) times the coupling squared.

What they do NOT establish
--------------------------
Anything at all about Born probabilities.  See README.md.
"""
